    "old": "it ends up acting like free water that can move into cells (Section 8.3).</p>",
    "new": "it ends up acting like free water that can move into cells (Section 8.3).</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Three red blood cells, each in a different salt solution. Left, hypotonic, 0.45 percent NaCl: blue arrows point from the solution into the cell; water moves in, and the cell is drawn swollen and round because it swells and may burst. Middle, isotonic, 0.9 percent NaCl (normal saline): pairs of arrows point both in and out, showing no net movement of water; the cell is a normal biconcave disc with a pale center. Right, hypertonic, 3 percent NaCl: arrows point out of the cell; water moves out, and the cell is drawn small with a spiky, crenated edge because it shrinks.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k1rbc}}/fig-p597148-rbc-tonicity.svg\" fileid=\"{{k1rbc}}\" /><figcaption>Figure 9.5.6: Red blood cells in hypotonic (0.45% NaCl), isotonic (0.9% NaCl, normal saline) and hypertonic (3% NaCl) solutions. Water moves toward the side with more dissolved particles: into the cell (it swells and may burst), in and out equally (normal biconcave shape), or out of the cell (it shrinks and crenates). (Diagram: Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
