    "old": "to protect its phenol groups from oxidation.</p>",
    "new": "to protect its phenol groups from oxidation.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Qualitative horizontal bar chart of how much of the lidocaine is in the neutral, uncharged amine form, R3N, the only form that crosses the nerve membrane, in three places. No numbers are given. In the cartridge, which is acidic at about pH 3.5 to 6.5, the bar is very short: almost all the drug is the charged, dissolved salt R3NH+ Cl-. In healthy tissue, buffered to pH 7.4, the bar is much longer: more of the drug is neutral R3N, which crosses the nerve membrane. In an abscess or inflamed tissue, where the pH is lower than 7.4, the bar is short again: more drug stays as R3NH+, so anesthesia is slower and weaker.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k1lido}}/fig-p597123-lidocaine-neutral.svg\" fileid=\"{{k1lido}}\" /><figcaption>Figure 7.3.2: Where lidocaine is in its neutral, membrane-crossing form (R<sub>3</sub>N). In the acidic cartridge almost all of it is the charged salt; at tissue pH 7.4 more of it is neutral and reaches the nerve; in an acidic abscess more stays charged, so anesthesia is slower and weaker. (Qualitative, not to scale.) (Diagram: Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
    "count": 1,
